---
layout: base.njk
pageClass: homepage
---

<main id="main">
	<!--hero section-->
	<section class="region" id="hero">
		<div class="flow wrapper">
			<h1>
				<span class="first-name sidebar">
					<span class="text-box-trim-both">Arpit</span>
				</span>
				<span class="last-name text-box-trim-both">
					<picture>
						<img
							src="/assets/img/arpit.jpg"
							alt=""
							eleventy:widths="100,200,400"
							sizes="120px"
						/>
					</picture>
					Agrawal
				</span>
			</h1>
			<div class="desc-container with-ghost-sidebar">
				<div class="text-box-trim-both">
					<p class="text-balance">
						Hi, I'm a <b>frontend engineer</b> based in Mumbai, India. For six years, I've run an independent practice, designing and building websites end to end. I specialise in front of the front end work: semantic HTML, resilient CSS, and accessibility.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!--about section-->
	<section class="region">
		<div class="wrapper">
			<div class="sidebar">
				<h2 id="about">About</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="flow text-box-trim-both">
				<!--origin story-->
				<p>
			  	In 2019, I was studying chartered accountancy when I watched my cousin design and build a website from scratch, writing code and seeing it take shape in real time. I knew I'd found the work I wanted to do. So I learned to build for the web.
				</p>
				<!--how I build-->
				<h3>How I Build</h3>
				<p>
			  	Since May 2020, I've designed and built websites for clients from the ground up, handling everything from prototyping in the browser to deployment. I write semantic HTML and resilient CSS that communicates the design intent to the browser, leaning on the cascade and intrinsic layouts instead of hardcoded breakpoints or magic numbers. The interface leverages the web platform (HTML, CSS, and JavaScript), with a framework on top only where it earns its place.
				</p>
				<!--why it matters-->
				<h3>Why I Build This Way</h3>
				<p>
					I build this way because it lasts, and because it includes more people. The platform outlasts every framework, so the HTML and CSS stay portable across frameworks and a change of stack doesn't mean a rewrite. What I ship stays accessible regardless of ability, device, browser, or connection speed.
				</p>
				<p>
					Performance is part of that, not a separate pass: I keep the payload small, and I treat a slow connection as a condition to support rather than an edge case to apologise for.
				</p>
				<!--what I'm looking for-->
				<h3>What I'm Looking For</h3>
				<p>
					I like working alongside developers and teams who see frontend the same way: a foundation worth building well, not a layer to rush past. I'm looking for a full-time role, based in Mumbai but open to relocation and to remote work.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!--projects section-->
	<section class="flow region">
		<div class="wrapper">
			<div class="sidebar">
				<h2 id="projects">Projects</h2>
			</div>
			<div>
				<p class="text-box-trim-both text-balance">
					A selection of websites I designed and built. Each one shaped by its
					purpose and the people it serves.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="project-list-container">
			<div class="project-list-scrollport with-ghost-sidebar">
				<div>{% include 'components/project-list.njk' %}</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!--contact section-->
	{% include 'components/contact.njk' %}
</main>
